      "year": 2019,
      "abstract": "This article examines some significant yet little-known early anthropological achievements in Italy. These include the world\u2019s first museum of anthropology, founded in 1869 by Paolo Mantegazza (1831\u20131910) at Florence (Firenze), Italy, where that same year he also established Italy\u2019s (and the world\u2019s) first cattedra (university professorship) of anthropology. Mantegazza sought to develop a unified \u201cscience of man,\u201d with a broad definition of the new discipline that brought together human physiological, ethnographic, and \u201ccomparative psychology\u201d collections within his new anthropology museum, later complemented by a companion \u201cpsychological\u201d museum. Even though Mantegazza\u2019s Florentine school of anthropology ended under Fascism, today the surviving Museum of Anthropology in Florence is still the repository of important ethnographic collections from early Italian traveler-explorers and other contributors. Their study was an important component of Mantegazza\u2019s science, which is receiving new attention by modern Italian anthropologists. [museum anthropology, evolution, pangenesis, history of anthropology, Italian anthropology] A New Science of Man To study the origin of man, to fix his natural position in the hierarchy of living things, to study also the changes brought about by climate, race, sex, nutrition, sickness; to study the varieties, races and different types of mankind, to classify them, to research their cross-breeding and human hybrids, to analyze the individual, to define and measure his forces; to study the different human groups in their customs, in their history, in their psychology, and to trace the natural history of each one; to attempt to delimit human capacity for advancement\u2014that is what this science proposes. (Mantegazza 1901, 4) This article aims to shed new light on Italy\u2019s historical legacy within anthropology and its potential relevance to museum anthropologists today. We revisit the intellectual and political context in which the world\u2019s first museum of anthropology was founded in Florence, Italy, by Paolo Mantegazza, describing the conceptual underpinnings of his early vision of a comprehensive science of man and the role his museum of anthropology played for that new science. We emphasize his opposition to idealist (or \u201csuperorganic\u201d) traditions that later came to dominate Italian anthropology, his anti-idealist position regarding evolutionary theory, and his reasons why this new science needed the museum. This summary also reviews the political implications of those positions in order to explain why the Florentine school of thought he founded was later virtually eliminated under Fascism. With his founding of the first museum of anthropology, in addition to creating and occupying the first cattedra or university professorship of anthropology (both in Florence, Italy, in 1869), Mantegazza was attempting to grapple not only with theories and concepts but also with the proper placement and systematization of growing numbers of human physical (mostly skeletal) and ethnographic collections that did not fit well within Italy\u2019s already-extensive scientific or artistic collections and museums. These early \u201canthropological\u201d collections are worldwide in scope. We introduce here as examples some important pre1869 collections brought back to Florence and other cities by Italian traveler-explorers. These collections still await proper study and appreciation for their historical and ethnographic value. Our article reflects the revived interest within Italy for the study of this period, especially the history of Italian anthropology and its peculiar character in the broader context of European and American anthropological traditions (Barth et al. 2005; Bernard 1980; Chiarelli 1993; museum anthropology Museum Anthropology, Vol. 42, Iss. 2, pp. 109\u201324 Published 2019. This article is a U.S. Government work and is in the public domain in the USA. Museum Anthropology published by Wiley Periodicals, Inc. on behalf of American Anthropological Association This is an open access article under the terms of the Creative Commons Attribution License, which permits use, distribution and reproduction in any medium, provided the original work is properly cited. DOI: 10.1111/muan.12209 Pogliano 1993; Roselli 2016; Saunders 1984). This scholarly retrospective complements the new approaches to an overall reassessment of ethnographic museum collections currently underway in Italy (Moggi Cecchi and Stanyon 2014) and the Vatican (Aigner and Mapelli 2015), where relevant archival collections are also located (Codignola 1991; Pizzorusso 1990). Italian anthropology has a precise beginning with the founding of the first university professorship in anthropology, within the Faculty of Philosophy and Letters at the University of Florence, under Paolo Mantegazza (Roselli 2016). Upon receiving the professorship in 1869, Mantegazza pursued a partial break with any previous philosophical uses of the term \u201canthropology\u201d (e.g., by Immanuel Kant) by attempting to transfer his cattedra to the Faculty of Biological Sciences and by founding his museum of anthropology in association with that biological faculty. In 1871, following the French example, Mantegazza founded the Societ a Italiana di Antropologia ed Etnologia, with its own journal, the Archivio per l\u2019Antropologia e la Etnologia. The words \u201ce la Psicologia Comparata\u201d (and Comparative Psychology) were added in 1878 (in use until 1911) to the name of the society and to the journal\u2019s title. The addition reflected Mantegazza\u2019s eclectic vision of the science of man and his plan to create a new \u201cpsychological museum\u201d as the companion institution to his museum of anthropology. Despite scholarly opposition, the Museo Psicologico was eventually approved by royal decree in 1889 and officially inaugurated in Florence in 1891 (Ciruzzi 1991; Pardini andMainardi 1991). Paolo Mantegazza and His Museums of Anthropology and Psychology Paolo Mantegazza was born on October 31, 1831, in Monza, Lombardy, the city he would later represent as deputy to the Italian Parliament from 1865 to 1876, then as a senator for many more years (Figure 1). After studying medicine for a year at the University of Pisa, in 1851 Mantegazza transferred to the University of Pavia to continue his medical schooling there. In 1854 he received his MD with a thesis entitled Fisiologia del piacere (Physiology of Pleasure), published that same year in Milano. In the preface to his book, the young doctor explained that \u201cthis study is a first step I am taking on a trail I have chosen to follow in my life; it is an essay on the method I intend to follow in the physiological study of moral man, to which I wish to dedicate my modest strengths\u201d (Mantegazza 1854, [2]). Mantegazza refers here to studying psychological and \u201cmoral\u201d or cultural/societal human variation grounded in the study of physiology. With respect to one related area of Mantegazza\u2019s studies, Sigusch (2008, 217\u201318) has argued that Mantegazza should be recognized as one of the first \u201csexual physiologists, sexual ethnologists, sexual empirical researchers, sexual hygienists, and also sexual theoreticians.\u201d Though Mantegazza\u2019s original training in medicine (including its component sciences, anatomy and physiology) would remain an underlying theme throughout his scientific career, he chose not to practice medicine. Rather, Mantegazza crossed scientific boundaries and increasingly explored the kindred fields of natural sciences as they related to the study of humankind, including racial morphology, ethnology, and comparative physiology and psychology. After receiving his medical doctorate, Mantegazza first Figure 1. Portrait of Paolo Mantegazza (1831\u20131910). Unnumbered plate, Archivio per l\u2019Antropologia e l\u2019Etnologia (v. 40 opposite p. 485), 1910, before obituaries by Modigliani (1910) andMochi (1910). paolo mantegazza\u2019s vision",
